  "optimizer_equity": {
    "method": "the optimizer in pipeline/s6_optimize.py selects segment and intervention pairs by cost effectiveness only, averted degree-minutes per dollar, with no SVI term anywhere in its objective or constraints, this analysis maps the funded set at each budget level in data/out/solutions.json back to segment SVI to see what the blind objective happens to produce, three views are reported, set membership at successive budgets, which saturates almost immediately because the cheapest intervention is cost effective nearly everywhere, a dollar weighted mean SVI curve across every budget level, which accounts for the fact that some interventions cost 50 to 60 times more than others per segment, and an explicit statement of how much that curve moves, because a single point on it is not a reportable effect size",
    "baseline_mean_svi_all_172_segments": 0.4075,
    "baseline_median_svi_all_172_segments": 0.2809,
    "n_distinct_svi_tract_values": 3,
    "saturation_budget_usd": 52500,
    "saturation_note": "budget level, if any, at which every one of the 172 segments already has at least one funded intervention, this happens quickly because a single street tree is only 300 USD. below this budget, comparing which segments are funded against the full segment set is a meaningful membership based equity check. at or above it, every segment is already funded, so a membership based comparison is vacuous, everyone is in the funded set regardless of SVI, only the dollar weighted curve remains informative above saturation, and even that keeps moving because additional spend concentrates on pricier interventions on a subset of the already-funded segments",
